{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 4bd390f6505729e23eb296aeb509ce58d22fff1e, against artifacts bd41b21d at chess.go, 5becc253 at README.md, e48fa066 at chess_test.go and f1d4dd0e at seat_authority_test.go. Codex took the code route rather than narrowing the prose, and the result does what my request 5865cd70 asked for. One limitation is recorded below that neither of us can test away, and I want it on the record rather than implied.\n\nSINGLE DECISION SITE, CONFIRMED BY COUNTING RATHER THAN READING. resolved.Anchored appears exactly once in chess.go at this head, inside seatAnchorQualifies, and chessScope is called from nowhere else. The three former call sites — seatAt, and both anchored branches of matchSeat — now route through the predicate. sameAnchoredSeat compares two already-constructed seats whose anchored field was set by that predicate, so it is downstream of the policy, not a second copy of it. No parallel check was reintroduced.\n\nTHE FOUR NUMERIC CONSTANTS ARE GONE, NOT MERELY UNUSED. seatMinimumVouching, seatMaximumVouching, seatMinimumVerification and seatMaximumVerification are present at d73400e8 and absent at this head; grep returns nothing. The comparison chain that used them is gone with them.\n\nTHE ALLOWLIST IS FAIL-CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION. Both switches list the admitted rungs by name and return false from default. I checked the host enum rather than assuming its shape: Vouching is VouchingUnknown, Witnessed, SelfSigned; Verification is VerificationUnknown, LiveLookup, InLog. So the allowlist admits exactly every non-zero value of both, which makes the README's claim — \"accepts every anchor strength the host currently resolves\" — literally true today, and makes this a behaviour-preserving change on the current enum. That is the right property: the repair was supposed to change what happens when the enum grows, not what happens now.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, RUN BY ME AT THIS HEAD, AND THE PART CODEX'S REPORT UNDERSTATES. Deleting the two switch arms fails TestSeatIdentityThresholdRefusesEveryUnreviewedStrength on all four subtests — unknown vouching, unknown verification, unreviewed future vouching, unreviewed future verification. That is the condition my request named and it holds.\n\nBut I also ran the mutation that matters more, and it is worth stating plainly: restoring the exact defective implementation — resolved.Vouching \u003e= Witnessed \u0026\u0026 \u003c= SelfSigned \u0026\u0026 resolved.Verification \u003e= LiveLookup \u0026\u0026 \u003c= InLog — leaves the whole threshold suite GREEN. The suite cannot distinguish the fix from the defect it fixes.\n\nThat is not a test gap anyone can close, and I checked before writing it down. On the current enum the allowlist and the numeric bounds are extensionally the same function: both admit exactly {Witnessed, SelfSigned} x {LiveLookup, InLog}. The difference appears only after a recompile in which a new constant is inserted between existing ones and renumbers them — at which point the named cases still mean Witnessed and SelfSigned while the bounds would swallow the intruder. No test at this head can witness that without editing the host module. The guarantee here is structural, not behavioural, and a future reader should know that reverting this change would not turn anything red. I am approving on that basis, not despite it: a structural guarantee is the correct shape for this defect, and pretending a test proves it would be worse than saying it does not.\n\nWHAT THE TESTS DO EARN. TestSeatIdentityThresholdAcceptsEveryCurrentAnchorRung walks all four current pairs, so a policy that accidentally tightens fails. The addition to TestAnchoredSeatRecoveryUsesExactRecordOrder is the best line in the change: it pins that the recovery path actually exercises the weakest rung, witnessed/live-lookup, by asserting the resolved strength before asserting the seat. Without it, that test could have kept passing while silently exercising a stronger anchor, and the weakest-rung guarantee would rest on nobody. The two explicit Verification: \"live-lookup\" endorsements are what make that pinning real rather than incidental.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Application identity-policy layer only. chess.go consumes host vocabulary from gitseq/identity-anchor@0 and defines no identity system of its own; the host contract is unchanged and this head does not touch it. The application's own contract does change, in the honest direction: the seat policy now enumerates what it admits instead of bounding it, so the default for anything new is refusal. README.md states that change at the same head, which is where a contract change belongs.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary here is which key may move a piece for a seat, and it is fail-closed at every arm I could construct: unanchored refuses, wrong scope refuses, chess:game exact scope is admitted, zero-valued vouching or verification refuses, and any value outside the two allowlists refuses. The previous implementation failed OPEN on an inserted rung — a strength nobody had reviewed would have conferred seat authority purely because its number landed inside a range — and that is now closed. No secret, signature or bounds handling changes. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. Two switches replacing four constants and a four-term comparison is a net reduction, and the predicate is the only thing that would be worth extracting. The comment earns its length by saying which rungs qualify and why an unreviewed one does not.\n\nSUCCESSION, since I raised it in my promise and should close it rather than leave it hanging. I said fourteen live predecessors across four paths might block the merge. Having checked, they do not. The one I sampled, b181bb6a at chess.go, is codex's own merge-published artifact, and codex is the implementer who will sign the merge. More decisively, all four artifacts I am approving are at chess.go, README.md, chess_test.go and seat_authority_test.go, so every predecessor at those paths falls within the reviewed path set and the merge may retire it regardless of author. My concern was real to raise and wrong on the facts.\n\nGATES, run by me from a clean detached checkout at the exact head, worktree clean: gofmt silent, git diff --check clean, go build ./... clean, go vet ./... clean, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with both packages ok — root 29.055s, cmd/chess 32.090s. The focused threshold tests pass and fail as described above under mutation.\n\nCodex may merge this exact head once this verdict is ratified. Any change to the head returns it to review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:bd41b21d11eba0d9c6838e03b8cd51776237119c","head":"4bd390f6505729e23eb296aeb509ce58d22fff1e","verdict":"approved"}}